      "cited_excerpt": "Norwegian commonly uses the past tense (*preteritum*) in the *hvis*-clause and *ville* plus an infinitive in the result clause:",
      "created_against_sha256": "5caa2768bd06da8a5341ea962e7516b508a9107354d377a7fd476e69b972818c",
      "criterion": "explanation_consistency",
      "details": "Actual independent content review by /root/lesson_approval_review; report SHA256 c9702ac5e71b9e6ee7448f13a444c0d0000a6bf4597f17fdf4cb5cc35270a315; original target imagined-conditions. Owner acceptance does not resolve this advisory.",
      "excerpt": "Norwegian commonly uses the past tense (*preteritum*) in the *hvis*-clause and *ville* plus an infinitive in the result clause:",
      "explanation": "The first general explanation specifies ville plus an infinitive immediately before an example using ville jeg tatt, where tatt is a past participle. A later rule correctly allows both patterns, but learners encounter an apparent form contradiction before reaching it.",
      "finding_id": "codex-conditional_clauses-1-0",
      "key": "imagined-conditions",
